COMMAND NAME: checkPerf

Verifies the baseline hardware performance of the specified hosts.

*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

checkPerf -c <config_file> -h <host_file> [-n <repeat_number>] 
[-t <0.01-0.99>] [--no-dd-test] [--no-stream-test] [-m <spec_file>] 
[-l <logfile>] [-p <ssh_port>] [-s | -x] [-v] [-d] [-?] 


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The checkPerf utility starts a session on each host that is specified 
in the given host file, and runs the following performance tests:

* Disk I/O Test (dd test)  To test the sequential throughput performance 
  of a logical disk or file system, the utility uses the dd command, which 
  is a standard UNIX utility. It times how long it takes to write and read 
  a large file to and from disk and calculates your disk I/O performance 
  in megabytes (MB) per second. By default, the file size that is used for 
  the test is calculated at two times the total RAM on the host. This 
  ensures that the test is truly testing disk I/O and not using the 
  memory cache.

* Memory Bandwidth Test (stream)  To test memory bandwidth, the utility 
  uses the STREAM benchmark program to measure sustainable memory 
  bandwidth (in MB/s). This tests that your system is not limited in 
  performance by the memory bandwidth of the system in relation to the 
  computational performance of the CPU. In applications where the data 
  set is large (as in Greenplum Database), low memory bandwidth is a major 
  performance issue. If memory bandwidth is significantly lower than the 
  theoretical bandwidth of the CPU, then it can cause the CPU to spend 
  significant amounts of time waiting for data to arrive from system memory.

The checkPerf utility requires two files in order to run - a host file 
that contains the names of all hosts included in the performance test, 
and a configuration file that has the testing parameters. A default 
configuration file is located at:

$MPPHOME/mppMgmt/doc/checkPerf_config_example

You can use this example configuration file as a basis for creating your 
own checkPerf configuration file. Most likely, you will only need to 
change the baseDir and DirectoryList parameters in this example file. 
The other parameter defaults are sufficient.

The checkPerf utility requires Python 2.3 or higher. Python is installed 
with most operating systems by default. If you have an older version of 
Python, you must upgrade to 2.3 or higher in order to use checkPerf.

The checkPerf utility will also exchange SSH keys between the hosts at 
the start of a session if you do not have a trusted host environment 
established already. This allows SSH connections to the remote hosts 
without a password prompt. If a user name is not specified in the host 
list file, then the currently logged in user will be used.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-h <host_file>

 Required. The name of a file that contains a list of hosts that will 
 participate in the performance test. The format of the host file is 
 as follows:

  username@host1
  username@host2
  ...
  username@hostN

 OR (if you want to connect as the current user)

  host1
  host2
  ...
  hostN

-c <config_file>

 Required. The configuration file used by checkPerf. An example 
 configuration file is located at: 
 $MPPHOME/mppMgmt/doc/checkPerf_config_example 
 Most likely, you will only need to change the baseDir and DirectoryList 
 parameters in this example file. The other parameter defaults are sufficient.

-n <repeat_number>

 Optional. The number of times to repeat the tests. This will run the 
 tests multiple times concurrently and then average the results. If not 
 specified, the tests are run once (or the number of times specified in 
 the configuration file RptCount parameter).

-t <tolerance_percentage>

 Optional. This is the tolerated percentage of variance allowed between 
 the test results of each host. The allowed value range is 0.01 to 0.99. 
 If the performance of one or more hosts varies by this percentage, then 
 the checkPerf output will report the detailed results for each host in 
 the test (otherwise only the average result is reported). If not 
 specified, the tolerated variance is 0.05 or 5% (or the number of  
 times specified in the configuration file Tolerance parameter).

--no-dd-test

 Optional. Passing this option will not run the disk I/O performance 
 test (dd command). 

--no-stream-test

 Optional. Passing this option will not run the sustained memory 
 bandwidth test (STREAM benchmark program).

-m <spec_file>

 Optional. This is the location of the Python configuration file used 
 by checkPerf. Syntax for this file is based on python module 
 configobj-4.3.2. If not specified, the 
 $MPPHOME/mppMgmt/bin/lib/checkPerf.spec default specification file is used.

-l <logfile>

 Creates a log file for checkPerf using the given path and file name. 
 Log files are rolled over at 6 MB, and up to 6 log files are kept 
 at a time.

-p ssh_port

 Optional. If you are using a different SSH port besides the default 
 port of 22, use this option to specify the SSH port number.

-s (disable key exchange)

 Optional. Disable the setup of trusted host key exchange. The default 
 behavior is to exchange SSH keys as the specified user between the 
 current host and the hosts in the host file.

-x (exchange keys with all hosts)

 Optional. Using this option will exchange SSH keys between the current 
 host and all hosts in the host file and also interchange keys between 
 the hosts listed in the host file.

-v (verbose mode)

 Optional. Verbose mode will report all commands sent to a host and 
 the output received.

-d (debug mode)

 Optional. Debug mode will report all commands sent to a host and the 
 output received plus additional debug messages.

-? (help)

 Displays the online help.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Run the checkPerf utility on the hosts listed in the host file named 
gp_hosts and using the default configuration file:

 checkPerf -h gp_hosts -c $MPPHOME/mppMgmt/doc/checkPerf_config_example

Example of the checkPerf configuration file parameters:

 Tolerance=0.05

 RptCount=1

 baseDir="/home/gpadmin"

 DirectoryList="/dbfast1", "/dbfast2", 

 BlockSize=8K

 FileSize=0

 Count=0

